    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Rebecca presents with lightheadedness and chest tightness. Vitals are BP 90/52 mmHg, HR 32 bpm, RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The ECG shows a constant, normal PR interval but with intermittent dropped QRS complexes in a fixed ratio. What is the correct classification of this block, and what is the most appropriate immediate step?', '["First-Degree AV Block; administer oral aspirin and discharge home with outpatient cardiology referral", "Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type II; immediately apply transcutaneous pacing pads and prepare for transvenous pacing due to high risk of progression to complete heart block", "Sinus arrhythmia; reassure the patient and advise avoiding caffeine and strenuous exercise", "Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach); administer intravenous atropine 1 mg and repeat the ECG in 30 minutes", "Third-Degree AV Block; perform immediate synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This ECG description defines Second-Degree AV Block Mobitz Type II, characterized by a constant PR interval with intermittent dropped QRS complexes. This block occurs below the level of the AV node (in the Bundle of His or bundle branches) and carries a high risk of sudden progression to complete (third-degree) heart block and severe hemodynamic collapse. The immediate management for symptomatic Mobitz Type II block is the application of transcutaneous pacing pads and preparation for transvenous pacing. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old female named Linda presents with syncope and severe lightheadedness. Vitals are BP 94/52 mmHg, HR 32 bpm, RR 20 rpm, and SpO2 99%. The ECG shows P waves occurring at a regular rate of 80 bpm, and QRS complexes occurring at a regular rate of 32 bpm, but there is no relationship or correlation between the P waves and the QRS complexes. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?', '["Administer a rapid infusion of 2 liters of normal saline to correct hypotension", "Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Initiate immediate transcutaneous pacing and prepare for transvenous pacing", "Prescribe oral metoprolol 25 mg to control the atrial rate", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push to restore AV conduction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ECG describes Third-Degree (Complete) AV Block, characterized by complete AV dissociation where the atria and ventricles beat independently. Complete heart block is a medical emergency that leads to low cardiac output and can progress to asystole. In symptomatic patients, the immediate treatment of choice is transcutaneous pacing (TCP) to maintain ventricular rate and perfusion, while arranging for emergent transvenous pacemaker insertion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
