    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old female named Stephanie presents with syncope and severe lightheadedness. Vitals are BP 88/54 mmHg, HR 44 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 100%. The ECG shows P waves occurring at a regular rate of 80 bpm, and QRS complexes occurring at a regular rate of 44 bpm, but there is no relationship or correlation between the P waves and the QRS complexes. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?', '["Administer a rapid infusion of 2 liters of normal saline to correct hypotension", "Prescribe oral metoprolol 25 mg to control the atrial rate", "Initiate immediate transcutaneous pacing and prepare for transvenous pacing", "Perform immediate unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push to restore AV conduction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ECG describes Third-Degree (Complete) AV Block, characterized by complete AV dissociation where the atria and ventricles beat independently. Complete heart block is a medical emergency that leads to low cardiac output and can progress to asystole. In symptomatic patients, the immediate treatment of choice is transcutaneous pacing (TCP) to maintain ventricular rate and perfusion, while arranging for emergent transvenous pacemaker insertion. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 36-year-old male named Edward presents to the Emergency Department triage room with palpitations and shortness of breath. Vitals are BP 80/58 mmHg, HR 162 bpm, RR 32 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The patient is confused and diaphoretic. The cardiac monitor shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 162 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?', '["Perform urgent synchronized cardioversion at 50\u2013100 Joules", "Administer diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg intravenously over 2 minutes", "Perform unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Perform carotid sinus massage or a modified Valsalva maneuver", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For any patient presenting with a tachyarrhythmia, the first clinical assessment is whether the patient is hemodynamically stable or unstable. Signs of instability include hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, or acute heart failure. In this case, the patient is unstable (hypotension, confusion). The correct treatment for unstable narrow-complex tachycardia is immediate synchronized cardioversion (starting at 50–100 Joules for regular narrow complex). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named George presents to the high-acuity area with palpitations and shortness of breath. Vitals are BP 86/60 mmHg, HR 162 bpm, RR 32 rpm, and SpO2 100%. The patient is confused and diaphoretic. The cardiac monitor shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 162 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?', '["Perform urgent synchronized cardioversion at 50\u2013100 Joules", "Perform carotid sinus massage or a modified Valsalva maneuver", "Perform unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Administer diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg intravenously over 2 minutes", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For any patient presenting with a tachyarrhythmia, the first clinical assessment is whether the patient is hemodynamically stable or unstable. Signs of instability include hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, or acute heart failure. In this case, the patient is unstable (hypotension, confusion). The correct treatment for unstable narrow-complex tachycardia is immediate synchronized cardioversion (starting at 50–100 Joules for regular narrow complex). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male named Kenneth presents to the Emergency Department triage room with palpitations and shortness of breath. Vitals are BP 86/50 mmHg, HR 185 bpm, RR 22 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The patient is confused and diaphoretic. The cardiac monitor shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 185 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?', '["Perform unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Perform carotid sinus massage or a modified Valsalva maneuver", "Perform urgent synchronized cardioversion at 50\u2013100 Joules", "Administer diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg intravenously over 2 minutes", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For any patient presenting with a tachyarrhythmia, the first clinical assessment is whether the patient is hemodynamically stable or unstable. Signs of instability include hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, or acute heart failure. In this case, the patient is unstable (hypotension, confusion). The correct treatment for unstable narrow-complex tachycardia is immediate synchronized cardioversion (starting at 50–100 Joules for regular narrow complex). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
