    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old male named Robert presents to the bedside monitor area with palpitations and shortness of breath. Vitals are BP 88/56 mmHg, HR 204 bpm, RR 24 rpm, and SpO2 99%. The patient is confused and diaphoretic. The cardiac monitor shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 204 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?', '["Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push", "Perform unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Administer diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg intravenously over 2 minutes", "Perform carotid sinus massage or a modified Valsalva maneuver", "Perform urgent synchronized cardioversion at 50\u2013100 Joules"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For any patient presenting with a tachyarrhythmia, the first clinical assessment is whether the patient is hemodynamically stable or unstable. Signs of instability include hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, or acute heart failure. In this case, the patient is unstable (hypotension, confusion). The correct treatment for unstable narrow-complex tachycardia is immediate synchronized cardioversion (starting at 50–100 Joules for regular narrow complex). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male named Steven presents to the high-acuity area with palpitations and shortness of breath. Vitals are BP 86/48 mmHg, HR 155 bpm, RR 32 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The patient is confused and diaphoretic. The cardiac monitor shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 155 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?', '["Perform carotid sinus massage or a modified Valsalva maneuver", "Perform unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push", "Perform urgent synchronized cardioversion at 50\u2013100 Joules", "Administer diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg intravenously over 2 minutes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For any patient presenting with a tachyarrhythmia, the first clinical assessment is whether the patient is hemodynamically stable or unstable. Signs of instability include hypotension, altered mental status, chest pain, or acute heart failure. In this case, the patient is unstable (hypotension, confusion). The correct treatment for unstable narrow-complex tachycardia is immediate synchronized cardioversion (starting at 50–100 Joules for regular narrow complex). (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 42-year-old female named Nancy presents to the Emergency Department triage room with rapid palpitations. Vitals are BP 134/78 mmHg, HR 185 bpm, RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The patient is alert, oriented, and denies chest pain or dyspnea. The ECG shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 185 bpm with no visible P waves. After an unsuccessful modified Valsalva maneuver, what is the next most appropriate step?', '["Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Administer intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes", "Administer intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush", "Administer adenosine 12 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stable Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT), most likely AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT), as evidenced by a regular narrow-complex tachycardia in a hemodynamically stable patient. The first step is vagal maneuvers (e.g., modified Valsalva). If vagal maneuvers fail, the next first-line therapy is intravenous adenosine. The initial dose is 6 mg rapid IV push through a large-bore peripheral line, immediately followed by a rapid saline flush. If unsuccessful, a second dose of 12 mg IV push can be administered. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named Christopher presents to the resuscitation bay with rapid palpitations. Vitals are BP 126/84 mmHg, HR 192 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The patient is alert, oriented, and denies chest pain or dyspnea. The ECG shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 192 bpm with no visible P waves. After an unsuccessful modified Valsalva maneuver, what is the next most appropriate step?', '["Administer adenosine 12 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush", "Administer intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Administer intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has stable Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT), most likely AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT), as evidenced by a regular narrow-complex tachycardia in a hemodynamically stable patient. The first step is vagal maneuvers (e.g., modified Valsalva). If vagal maneuvers fail, the next first-line therapy is intravenous adenosine. The initial dose is 6 mg rapid IV push through a large-bore peripheral line, immediately followed by a rapid saline flush. If unsuccessful, a second dose of 12 mg IV push can be administered. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
