    (v_topic_id, 'A 42-year-old female named Stephanie presents to the high-acuity area with rapid palpitations. Vitals are BP 130/82 mmHg, HR 178 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 96%. The patient is alert, oriented, and denies chest pain or dyspnea. The ECG shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 178 bpm with no visible P waves. After an unsuccessful modified Valsalva maneuver, what is the next most appropriate step?', '["Administer adenosine 12 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush", "Administer adenosine 6 mg rapid intravenous push, followed by a saline flush", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Administer intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes", "Administer intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has stable Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT), most likely AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT), as evidenced by a regular narrow-complex tachycardia in a hemodynamically stable patient. The first step is vagal maneuvers (e.g., modified Valsalva). If vagal maneuvers fail, the next first-line therapy is intravenous adenosine. The initial dose is 6 mg rapid IV push through a large-bore peripheral line, immediately followed by a rapid saline flush. If unsuccessful, a second dose of 12 mg IV push can be administered. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old female named Sharon presents with a three-day history of palpitations and mild fatigue. Vitals are BP 138/78 mmHg, HR 185 bpm (irregular), RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The ECG reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves and narrow QRS complexes, consistent with Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response. The patient is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy?', '["Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Rate control with intravenous diltiazem or metoprolol, and assessment of stroke risk using the CHA2DS2-VASc score", "Administration of intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to block the AV node", "Immediate initiation of oral warfarin and discharge home without rate control", "Administration of intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes followed by heparin infusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For hemodynamically stable Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response (RVR), the primary goal in the Emergency Department is rate control rather than immediate rhythm control, especially if the duration of AFib is unknown or >48 hours (due to risk of thromboembolism). First-line agents include calcium channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem) or beta-blockers (e.g., metoprolol). Additionally, long-term stroke risk must be evaluated using the CHA2DS2-VASc score to determine the need for anticoagulation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female named Susan presents with a three-day history of palpitations and mild fatigue. Vitals are BP 120/78 mmHg, HR 170 bpm (irregular), RR 18 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The ECG reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves and narrow QRS complexes, consistent with Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response. The patient is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy?', '["Administration of intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to block the AV node", "Rate control with intravenous diltiazem or metoprolol, and assessment of stroke risk using the CHA2DS2-VASc score", "Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Administration of intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes followed by heparin infusion", "Immediate initiation of oral warfarin and discharge home without rate control"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For hemodynamically stable Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response (RVR), the primary goal in the Emergency Department is rate control rather than immediate rhythm control, especially if the duration of AFib is unknown or >48 hours (due to risk of thromboembolism). First-line agents include calcium channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem) or beta-blockers (e.g., metoprolol). Additionally, long-term stroke risk must be evaluated using the CHA2DS2-VASc score to determine the need for anticoagulation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
