    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male named Ronald presents with a three-day history of palpitations and mild fatigue. Vitals are BP 122/84 mmHg, HR 162 bpm (irregular), RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The ECG reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves and narrow QRS complexes, consistent with Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response. The patient is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy?', '["Rate control with intravenous diltiazem or metoprolol, and assessment of stroke risk using the CHA2DS2-VASc score", "Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Administration of intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes followed by heparin infusion", "Administration of intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to block the AV node", "Immediate initiation of oral warfarin and discharge home without rate control"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For hemodynamically stable Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response (RVR), the primary goal in the Emergency Department is rate control rather than immediate rhythm control, especially if the duration of AFib is unknown or >48 hours (due to risk of thromboembolism). First-line agents include calcium channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem) or beta-blockers (e.g., metoprolol). Additionally, long-term stroke risk must be evaluated using the CHA2DS2-VASc score to determine the need for anticoagulation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
