    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old female named Betty presents with a three-day history of palpitations and mild fatigue. Vitals are BP 122/78 mmHg, HR 162 bpm (irregular), RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The ECG reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves and narrow QRS complexes, consistent with Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response. The patient is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management strategy?', '["Rate control with intravenous diltiazem or metoprolol, and assessment of stroke risk using the CHA2DS2-VASc score", "Immediate initiation of oral warfarin and discharge home without rate control", "Administration of intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes followed by heparin infusion", "Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to restore sinus rhythm", "Administration of intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to block the AV node"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For hemodynamically stable Atrial Fibrillation with Rapid Ventricular Response (RVR), the primary goal in the Emergency Department is rate control rather than immediate rhythm control, especially if the duration of AFib is unknown or >48 hours (due to risk of thromboembolism). First-line agents include calcium channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem) or beta-blockers (e.g., metoprolol). Additionally, long-term stroke risk must be evaluated using the CHA2DS2-VASc score to determine the need for anticoagulation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male named Andrew presents to the high-acuity area with rapid palpitations and lightheadedness. Vitals are BP 122/72 mmHg, HR 204 bpm (irregular), RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 96%. The ECG shows an irregularly irregular wide-complex tachycardia with a ventricular rate fluctuating between 180 and 240 bpm. The QRS morphology varies dynamically. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacological treatment?', '["Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to control ventricular rate", "Intravenous digoxin 0.5 mg to increase vagal tone and slow conduction", "Intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to slow AV conduction", "Intravenous procainamide 17 mg/kg infusion over 20\u201330 minutes, or ibutilide", "Intravenous diltiazem 20 mg over 2 minutes to control ventricular rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with pre-excited atrial fibrillation in the setting of Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome. This is characterized by an irregularly irregular, extremely rapid (often >200 bpm), wide-complex tachycardia with variable QRS complexes. In WPW-AFib, AV nodal blocking agents (Adenosine, Beta-blockers, Calcium channel blockers, and Digoxin - ABCD) are strictly contraindicated because blocking the AV node will divert all conduction down the accessory pathway, potentially precipitating ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest. For stable patients, the treatment of choice is intravenous procainamide or ibutilide. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Anna presents to the resuscitation bay with rapid palpitations and lightheadedness. Vitals are BP 122/86 mmHg, HR 192 bpm (irregular), RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 96%. The ECG shows an irregularly irregular wide-complex tachycardia with a ventricular rate fluctuating between 180 and 240 bpm. The QRS morphology varies dynamically. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacological treatment?', '["Intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push to slow AV conduction", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to control ventricular rate", "Intravenous digoxin 0.5 mg to increase vagal tone and slow conduction", "Intravenous procainamide 17 mg/kg infusion over 20\u201330 minutes, or ibutilide", "Intravenous diltiazem 20 mg over 2 minutes to control ventricular rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with pre-excited atrial fibrillation in the setting of Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome. This is characterized by an irregularly irregular, extremely rapid (often >200 bpm), wide-complex tachycardia with variable QRS complexes. In WPW-AFib, AV nodal blocking agents (Adenosine, Beta-blockers, Calcium channel blockers, and Digoxin - ABCD) are strictly contraindicated because blocking the AV node will divert all conduction down the accessory pathway, potentially precipitating ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest. For stable patients, the treatment of choice is intravenous procainamide or ibutilide. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
