    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old female named Melissa presents with chest discomfort and palpitations. Vitals are BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 155 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and SpO2 100%. The ECG reveals a regular, monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia at 155 bpm. The patient is alert, oriented, and denies dyspnea or lightheadedness. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?', '["Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes, or intravenous procainamide", "Intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push", "Intravenous lidocaine 100 mg push over 1 minute", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient presents with stable monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (VT), as evidenced by a regular wide-complex tachycardia in a hemodynamically stable patient. First-line pharmacological management includes intravenous amiodarone or intravenous procainamide (which is often preferred in stable patient populations without prolonged QT or severe heart failure). Lidocaine is an alternative but has lower efficacy. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
