    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Karen presents with chest discomfort and palpitations. Vitals are BP 130/86 mmHg, HR 178 bpm, RR 14 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The ECG reveals a regular, monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia at 178 bpm. The patient is alert, oriented, and denies dyspnea or lightheadedness. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?', '["Intravenous adenosine 6 mg rapid push", "Intravenous amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes, or intravenous procainamide", "Intravenous lidocaine 100 mg push over 1 minute", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes", "Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient presents with stable monomorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (VT), as evidenced by a regular wide-complex tachycardia in a hemodynamically stable patient. First-line pharmacological management includes intravenous amiodarone or intravenous procainamide (which is often preferred in stable patient populations without prolonged QT or severe heart failure). Lidocaine is an alternative but has lower efficacy. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female named Karen is brought to the resuscitation bay with severe chest pain and dizziness. Vitals are BP 88/48 mmHg, HR 170 bpm, RR 26 rpm, and SpO2 97%. The cardiac monitor shows monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 170 bpm. The patient is poorly responsive to commands. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Perform immediate vagal maneuvers and administer adenosine 6 mg IV", "Unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules", "Intravenous push of amiodarone 300 mg over 1 minute", "Initiate high-quality chest compressions and prepare for intubation", "Urgent synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules, after considering brief sedation if feasible"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has unstable Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) with a pulse (evidenced by hypotension, altered mental status, and chest pain). The immediate treatment of choice is urgent synchronized cardioversion (starting at 100 Joules for monomorphic VT). Synchronization prevents the shock from being delivered during the vulnerable T-wave phase, avoiding the induction of ventricular fibrillation. If the QRS rhythm is regular and monomorphic, we synchronize. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
