    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old male named Michael is brought to the resuscitation bay with severe chest pain and dizziness. Vitals are BP 94/54 mmHg, HR 178 bpm, RR 28 rpm, and SpO2 98%. The cardiac monitor shows monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 178 bpm. The patient is poorly responsive to commands. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate high-quality chest compressions and prepare for intubation", "Urgent synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules, after considering brief sedation if feasible", "Intravenous push of amiodarone 300 mg over 1 minute", "Perform immediate vagal maneuvers and administer adenosine 6 mg IV", "Unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 Joules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has unstable Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) with a pulse (evidenced by hypotension, altered mental status, and chest pain). The immediate treatment of choice is urgent synchronized cardioversion (starting at 100 Joules for monomorphic VT). Synchronization prevents the shock from being delivered during the vulnerable T-wave phase, avoiding the induction of ventricular fibrillation. If the QRS rhythm is regular and monomorphic, we synchronize. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male named Eric suddenly collapses in the Emergency Department. He is unresponsive, pulseless, and apneic. The cardiac monitor shows ventricular fibrillation. CPR is immediately initiated, and the defibrillator is charged. What is the next most appropriate action?', '["Administer epinephrine 1 mg intravenously before delivering any shocks", "Deliver an immediate unsynchronized shock (defibrillation) at 200 Joules biphasic, then resume chest compressions immediately", "Administer amiodarone 300 mg intravenously over 10 minutes", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to coordinate with the QRS complex", "Intubate the patient and obtain arterial blood gas values before defibrillation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) or Ventricular Fibrillation (VF), the single most critical intervention is immediate defibrillation (unsynchronized shock). Biphasic defibrillators should deliver the shock at the manufacturer''s recommended dose (typically 120–200 Joules). Following the shock, chest compressions (CPR) must be resumed immediately for 2 minutes before checking the rhythm or pulse. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
