    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old male named John suddenly collapses in the Emergency Department. He is unresponsive, pulseless, and apneic. The cardiac monitor shows ventricular fibrillation. CPR is immediately initiated, and the defibrillator is charged. What is the next most appropriate action?', '["Intubate the patient and obtain arterial blood gas values before defibrillation", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to coordinate with the QRS complex", "Deliver an immediate unsynchronized shock (defibrillation) at 200 Joules biphasic, then resume chest compressions immediately", "Administer epinephrine 1 mg intravenously before delivering any shocks", "Administer amiodarone 300 mg intravenously over 10 minutes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) or Ventricular Fibrillation (VF), the single most critical intervention is immediate defibrillation (unsynchronized shock). Biphasic defibrillators should deliver the shock at the manufacturer''s recommended dose (typically 120–200 Joules). Following the shock, chest compressions (CPR) must be resumed immediately for 2 minutes before checking the rhythm or pulse. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named Brian suddenly collapses in the Emergency Department. He is unresponsive, pulseless, and apneic. The cardiac monitor shows ventricular fibrillation. CPR is immediately initiated, and the defibrillator is charged. What is the next most appropriate action?', '["Administer amiodarone 300 mg intravenously over 10 minutes", "Perform synchronized cardioversion at 200 Joules to coordinate with the QRS complex", "Intubate the patient and obtain arterial blood gas values before defibrillation", "Deliver an immediate unsynchronized shock (defibrillation) at 200 Joules biphasic, then resume chest compressions immediately", "Administer epinephrine 1 mg intravenously before delivering any shocks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) or Ventricular Fibrillation (VF), the single most critical intervention is immediate defibrillation (unsynchronized shock). Biphasic defibrillators should deliver the shock at the manufacturer''s recommended dose (typically 120–200 Joules). Following the shock, chest compressions (CPR) must be resumed immediately for 2 minutes before checking the rhythm or pulse. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 22; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male named Donald presents with recurrent syncope. While attached to the cardiac monitor, the patient has an episode of rapid, irregular wide-complex tachycardia where the QRS complexes appear to twist around the baseline (polymorphic ventricular tachycardia). The patient is hemodynamically stable. An ECG obtained earlier showed a corrected QT (QTc) interval of 525 ms. Which of the following is the first-line therapy to prevent recurrence?', '["Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Intravenous amiodarone 150 mg push over 10 minutes", "Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate 2 grams push, followed by a maintenance infusion", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes", "Intravenous lidocaine 100 mg push over 1 minute"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Torsades de Pointes (TdP), a form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia characterized by the twisting of QRS complexes around the baseline, occurring in the setting of a prolonged QT interval (QTc > 500 ms). The first-line treatment for stable TdP is intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 grams IV), which stabilizes the myocardial membrane. Potassium should also be aggressively replete to a target of >4.5 mEq/L. Amiodarone is strictly contraindicated. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old male named Ryan presents with recurrent syncope. While attached to the cardiac monitor, the patient has an episode of rapid, irregular wide-complex tachycardia where the QRS complexes appear to twist around the baseline (polymorphic ventricular tachycardia). The patient is hemodynamically stable. An ECG obtained earlier showed a corrected QT (QTc) interval of 540 ms. Which of the following is the first-line therapy to prevent recurrence?', '["Immediate synchronized cardioversion at 100 Joules", "Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate 2 grams push, followed by a maintenance infusion", "Intravenous amiodarone 150 mg push over 10 minutes", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push over 2 minutes", "Intravenous lidocaine 100 mg push over 1 minute"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Torsades de Pointes (TdP), a form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia characterized by the twisting of QRS complexes around the baseline, occurring in the setting of a prolonged QT interval (QTc > 500 ms). The first-line treatment for stable TdP is intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 grams IV), which stabilizes the myocardial membrane. Potassium should also be aggressively replete to a target of >4.5 mEq/L. Amiodarone is strictly contraindicated. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 2; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 15)'),
